"""Section III: method (equations are numbered in order of appearance)."""

METHOD = [
("h1", "III. PROPOSED METHOD"),
("h2", "A. Problem Formulation"),
("p", "A video $V=\\{I_r\\}_{r=1}^{R}$ of $R$ raw frames carries a label $y\\in\\{1,\\dots,C\\}$. Frames are addressed through a candidate lattice $c_1<c_2<\\dots<c_T$ of raw indices spread uniformly over the clip, with normalized timestamps $\\tau_j=(j-1)/(T-1)$. A frozen image encoder $\\Phi$ maps a frame to one class token and $N$ patch tokens of width $D$. Two budgets are fixed in advance: $K$ encoder calls per video and $B$ patch tokens admitted into the temporal model, together with one class token and one context token per selected frame. The recognition problem is therefore"),
("eq", "\\min_{\\theta,\\phi}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{(V,y)}\\left[\\ell\\left(f_{\\theta}\\left(\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathcal{S}},\\boldsymbol{\\tau}\\right),y\\right)\\right]\\quad \\mathrm{s.t.}\\ \\ \\left|\\mathcal{J}\\right|=K,\\ \\left|\\mathcal{S}\\right|=B", "prob"),
("p", "where $\\mathcal{J}=\\{j_1<\\dots<j_K\\}$ is the set of selected lattice positions, $\\mathcal{X}_{\\mathcal{S}}$ is the set of encoder tokens retained by the allocation $\\mathcal{S}\\subset\\{1,\\dots,K\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$, $\\boldsymbol{\\tau}$ collects the timestamps of the selected frames, $f_{\\theta}$ is the temporal model with parameters $\\theta$, $\\phi$ denotes the parameters of the token scorer and $\\ell$ is the classification loss. The constraint on $\\mathcal{J}$ has to be met before $\\Phi$ runs, because the purpose of selection is to avoid encoding the other $T-K$ candidates. Fig. {fig:arch} shows the resulting pipeline; the upper row is computed from pixels alone and the lower row is trained."),
("h2", "B. Camera-Compensated Pixel Motion Probe"),
("p", "Each raw frame is converted to a grey image $g_r$ of $56\\times56$ pixels after the same centre crop used by the encoder. The image is divided into an $8\\times8$ grid of cells $\\Omega_n$, aligned with the encoder patch grid, and the absolute temporal difference is averaged inside every cell:"),
("eq", "e_{r,n}=\\frac{1}{\\left|\\Omega_{n}\\right|}\\sum_{p\\in\\Omega_{n}}\\left|g_{r}(p)-g_{r-1}(p)\\right|", "cell"),
("p", "Frame differencing responds to camera shake and panning as strongly as to object motion. Because a global translation raises the energy of almost every cell, the per-frame median across cells is a robust estimate of that common component, and only the excess is kept:"),
("eq", "\\tilde{e}_{r,n}=\\max\\left(0,\\ e_{r,n}-\\operatorname{med}_{n'}\\,e_{r,n'}\\right)", "resid"),
("p", "Residual energy is then accumulated between consecutive lattice points, so that a burst falling between two candidates is still registered. Many scenes also contain motion that never stops, such as foliage, screens, escalators or the slow drift of a hand-held camera; this persistent level is removed per region by a temporal median before the regions are summed into a scalar velocity:"),
("eq", "E_{j,n}=\\sum_{r=c_{j-1}+1}^{c_{j}}\\tilde{e}_{r,n}", "acc"),
("eq", "\\hat{E}_{j,n}=\\max\\left(0,\\ E_{j,n}-\\operatorname{med}_{j'}E_{j',n}\\right),\\qquad v_{j}=\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\hat{E}_{j,n}", "lat"),
("p", "The probe costs a few hundred operations per pixel of a $56\\times56$ image, three to four orders of magnitude below one pass of the encoder (Section V-F), and it needs no training."),
("h2", "C. Coverage-Floored Velocity-Quantile Sampling"),
("p", "Sampling frames in proportion to velocity concentrates them where the scene changes, but a pure proportional rule can place all $K$ samples inside one burst and leave long stretches of the video unseen. The sampler therefore draws from a mixture of a velocity term and a uniform floor,"),
("eq", "p_{j}=(1-\\rho)\\frac{v_{j}^{\\gamma}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{T}v_{i}^{\\gamma}}+\\frac{\\rho}{T},\\qquad F_{j}=\\sum_{i=1}^{j}p_{i}", "dens"),
("p", "where $\\rho\\in[0,1]$ sets the share of the budget reserved for coverage, $\\gamma>0$ sharpens or flattens the velocity profile and $F_j$ is the cumulative distribution over the lattice. The defaults $\\rho=0.2$ and $\\gamma=2$, as well as the use of the temporal median in {eq:lat}, were fixed on 200 separately generated TRACE-10 videos, whose random seeds do not overlap with the benchmark, by maximizing the burst hit rate of Section IV-E before any network was trained. The $k$-th frame is the lattice position at which $F$ first reaches the $k$-th stratified quantile,"),
("eq", "j_{k}=\\min\\left\\{j:F_{j}\\geq\\frac{k-1+u_{k}}{K}\\right\\},\\qquad u_{k}\\sim\\mathcal{U}(0,1)", "quant"),
("p", "with $u_k=1/2$ at inference and random offsets during training, which acts as temporal augmentation. Coincident indices are separated by a monotone projection that enforces $j_1<\\dots<j_K$. The setting $\\rho=1$ recovers segment-based uniform sampling and $\\rho=0$ with $\\gamma=1$ and uncompensated energy recovers the cumulative-motion rule of [@mgsampler]."),
("p", "*Proposition 1.* Let $\\Delta\\tau_{\\max}$ be the largest timestamp difference between consecutive samples and let $\\mathcal{B}$ be any contiguous set of lattice positions with velocity share $m_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{B}}v_j^{\\gamma}/\\sum_{i}v_i^{\\gamma}$. Before the monotone projection,"),
("eq", "\\Delta\\tau_{\\max}\\leq\\frac{1}{T-1}\\left(\\frac{T}{\\rho K}+1\\right),\\qquad n_{\\mathcal{B}}\\geq K\\left((1-\\rho)m_{\\mathcal{B}}+\\frac{\\rho\\left|\\mathcal{B}\\right|}{T}\\right)-1", "bound"),
("p", "where $n_{\\mathcal{B}}$ counts the samples that fall in $\\mathcal{B}$ and the first bound refers to inference ($u_k=1/2$). *Proof sketch.* Consecutive quantile targets differ by $1/K$ in $F$, while every lattice step raises $F$ by at least $\\rho/T$; hence no more than $T/(\\rho K)+1$ steps separate two samples (random offsets during training at most double the first term). For the second bound, the positions in $\\mathcal{B}$ own an interval of $F$ of length $P_{\\mathcal{B}}=(1-\\rho)m_{\\mathcal{B}}+\\rho|\\mathcal{B}|/T$, and an interval of that length contains at least $KP_{\\mathcal{B}}-1$ points of the grid $\\{(k-1+u_k)/K\\}$. Under uniform sampling the same burst receives about $K|\\mathcal{B}|/T$ samples, so the ratio $m_{\\mathcal{B}}T/|\\mathcal{B}|$ is the enrichment factor of a burst whose velocity exceeds the clip average. The first bound is what separates this sampler from pure motion-proportional rules: for $\\rho=0.2$, $K=8$ and $T=48$, no gap can exceed about $0.66$ of the clip, whereas with $\\rho=0$ the gap is unbounded. Fig. {fig:sampler} shows the probe signal, the two cumulative distributions and the resulting frame choices on one video drawn from the TRACE-10 generator outside the benchmark splits."),
("h2", "D. Cross-Frame Token Budget"),
("p", "After encoding, each selected frame $k$ provides a class token $\\mathbf{x}_{k,0}$ and patch tokens $\\mathbf{x}_{k,n}$, $n=1,\\dots,N$. Every patch receives a score that adds three sources of evidence:"),
("eq", "s_{k,n}=w_{m}\\,\\mathcal{Z}\\left(\\log\\left(1+\\hat{E}_{j_{k},n}\\right)\\right)+w_{n}\\,\\mathcal{Z}\\left(1-\\cos\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{k,n},\\mathbf{x}_{k-1,n}\\right)\\right)+g_{\\phi}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{k,n}\\right)", "score"),
("p", "Here $\\mathcal{Z}(\\cdot)$ standardizes a quantity over all $KN$ patches of the clip, the first term is the probe energy of the patch at its own lattice position, the second is feature novelty with respect to the same patch in the previously selected frame ($\\mathbf{x}_{0,n}\\equiv\\mathbf{x}_{1,n}$), $g_{\\phi}$ is a two-layer perceptron and $w_m$, $w_n$ are learned scalars initialized to one. The decisive design choice is that the top-$B$ operation is taken over the whole clip instead of frame by frame,"),
("eq", "\\mathcal{S}=\\operatorname{TopB}\\left(\\left\\{s_{k,n}\\right\\}_{k\\leq K,\\,n\\leq N}\\right),\\qquad B=\\beta KN-K", "topb"),
("p", "so a frame inside a burst may keep most of its patches while a near-duplicate frame keeps none; $\\beta$ is the retained fraction and the $K$ subtracted tokens pay for the context tokens defined below. Because top-$B$ has no useful gradient, the retained tokens are multiplied by a straight-through gate [@ste],"),
("eq", "\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k,n}=\\mathbf{x}_{k,n}\\left(1+\\sigma\\left(s_{k,n}\\right)-\\operatorname{sg}\\left[\\sigma\\left(s_{k,n}\\right)\\right]\\right),\\qquad(k,n)\\in\\mathcal{S}", "gate"),
("p", "where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function and $\\operatorname{sg}$ stops the gradient. The forward value equals $\\mathbf{x}_{k,n}$, while the backward pass sends $\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial s_{k,n}$ to $\\phi$, $w_m$ and $w_n$ (dashed path in Fig. {fig:arch}). Dropped patches are not discarded entirely. Their mean forms one context token per frame, which keeps a summary of the static scene,"),
("eq", "\\mathbf{c}_{k}=\\frac{1}{\\mu_{k}}\\sum_{n:(k,n)\\notin\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbf{x}_{k,n},\\qquad\\mu_{k}=\\left|\\left\\{n:(k,n)\\notin\\mathcal{S}\\right\\}\\right|", "ctx"),
("p", "and the attention logits receive the logarithm of each token's mass so that a context token standing for many patches is weighted accordingly [@tome]:"),
("eq", "\\mathbf{A}=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{Q}\\mathbf{K}^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_{h}}}+\\mathbf{1}\\left(\\log\\boldsymbol{\\mu}\\right)^{\\top}\\right)", "pattn"),
("p", "where $\\mathbf{Q}$ and $\\mathbf{K}$ are query and key matrices, $d_h$ is the head width and $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ holds the mass of every token (one for ordinary tokens, $\\mu_k$ for context tokens)."),
("h2", "E. Sampling-Geometry Encoding"),
("p", "After adaptive sampling, the slot index $k$ no longer tells the network when a frame was taken. The encoding therefore uses the continuous timestamp of each selected frame through a bank of sinusoids,"),
("eq", "\\boldsymbol{\\theta}(\\tau)=\\left[\\sin\\left(2^{f}\\pi\\tau\\right),\\ \\cos\\left(2^{f}\\pi\\tau\\right)\\right]_{f=0}^{5}", "four"),
("p", "and adds the local sampling interval on a logarithmic scale, normalized so that it is zero when the spacing equals the uniform spacing $1/K$:"),
("eq", "\\Delta_{k}=\\tau_{j_{k}}-\\tau_{j_{k-1}},\\qquad\\ell_{k}=\\frac{1}{4}\\log\\left(K\\Delta_{k}\\right)", "gap"),
("p", "Timestamps alone tell where a frame lies but not how fast its content changes. A finite-difference velocity in feature space, scaled by the same interval, supplies that information:"),
("eq", "\\mathbf{u}_{k,n}=\\frac{\\mathbf{x}_{k,n}-\\mathbf{x}_{k-1,n}}{K\\Delta_{k}}", "vel"),
("p", "Two clips that show the same displacement over different durations now produce different $\\ell_k$ and different velocity magnitudes, which an index encoding cannot represent. Timing is not always informative, however. On trimmed clips whose labels depend mostly on appearance, absolute timestamps add variation that the network can fit without benefit. The geometry terms therefore enter through learned scalar gates $\\alpha_{\\tau}$ and $\\alpha_{u}$, initialized at zero, on top of an ordinary order embedding $\\mathbf{o}_k$ of the slot index. The input embedding of a retained patch is"),
("eq", "\\mathbf{h}_{k,n}=\\mathbf{W}\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{k,n}+\\mathbf{p}_{n}+\\mathbf{o}_{k}+\\alpha_{\\tau}\\left(\\mathbf{W}_{\\tau}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\left(\\tau_{j_{k}}\\right)+\\mathbf{W}_{\\Delta}\\boldsymbol{\\theta}\\left(\\ell_{k}\\right)\\right)+\\alpha_{u}\\mathbf{W}_{u}\\mathbf{u}_{k,n}", "emb"),
("p", "where $\\mathbf{W}$, $\\mathbf{W}_{\\tau}$, $\\mathbf{W}_{\\Delta}$ and $\\mathbf{W}_{u}$ are learned projections and $\\mathbf{p}_n$ is a learned spatial embedding. At initialization the model therefore behaves like an index-encoded transformer and admits timing information only to the extent that it lowers the training loss. This gating was chosen on validation data before any test evaluation: an ungated version reached the same validation accuracy on TRACE-10 but 1.5 points less on HMDB51 (Section V-C reports both on the test splits). Class tokens use the same form with their own spatial embedding; context tokens omit the velocity term."),
("h2", "F. Temporal Reasoning and Training Objective"),
("p", "The $1+2K+B$ embedded tokens, consisting of a learned read-out token, $K$ class tokens, $K$ context tokens and $B$ patch tokens, pass through $L$ pre-norm transformer blocks [@attention] that use the attention of {eq:pattn}. The read-out state $\\mathbf{z}_0^{(L)}$ is classified and trained with label-smoothed cross-entropy,"),
("eq", "\\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\mathbf{W}_{c}\\operatorname{LN}\\left(\\mathbf{z}_{0}^{(L)}\\right)\\right),\\qquad\\mathcal{L}=-\\sum_{c=1}^{C}\\left((1-\\epsilon)\\mathbb{1}[c=y]+\\frac{\\epsilon}{C}\\right)\\log\\hat{y}_{c}", "loss"),
("p", "with $\\epsilon=0.1$. The encoder $\\Phi$ and the sampler receive no gradient; the scorer, the encoding projections and the transformer are trained jointly. Algorithms 1 and 2 summarize training and inference."),
]

